# Clean Code (baseline)

Universal principles that apply regardless of language or framework.

## Core principles
- **KISS** — simplest solution that works.
- **DRY** — extract only after 2–3 real repetitions, not speculative.
- **YAGNI** — no abstractions before a real caller exists.
- **Readability first** — self-documenting names beat comments.

## Immutability
- Return new objects/arrays — never mutate inputs.
- `const` by default; `let` only when reassignment is clearest.
- Pure functions where possible; isolate side effects.

## Naming
- `camelCase` vars/funcs, `PascalCase` types, `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE` consts.
- Booleans start with `is`/`has`/`should`/`can`.
- Names describe intent, not implementation.

## File & function size
- 200–400 lines per file (800 hard ceiling). Many small files > few large.
- ~50 lines per function; one responsibility; early returns over nesting.
- No magic numbers — name every meaningful constant.

## Error handling & types
- Handle errors explicitly at every layer — never silently swallow.
- Validate all input at system boundaries (HTTP, DB, file, user).
- No `any` — use `unknown` and narrow. Infer types from schemas.
